== prd/prd  path knowledge/prd.md  rev e3116b43b6f5
Lens scope: The line, from both sides (unreviewed)
  Non-Goals is where a PRD earns its keep, and it is the section that decays first.
  Does it name the thing a reasonable reader would assume is in scope? "Not this
  product, ever" and "not this version, and here is what would change that" are
  different claims — the second belongs under Deferred.
Lens journeys: Journeys reach capabilities (unreviewed)
  Every journey should have capabilities that make it possible, and every capability
  should serve a journey. A capability no journey needs is either load-bearing for a
  reason nobody wrote down, or it is not needed. A journey that only ever ends well
  cannot exercise the failure handling the product also has to do.
Lens metrics: Measurable, dated, and guarded (unreviewed)
  A success metric needs a number, a date and a source of truth. Then ask the harder
  question: what could someone break while moving it? That answer is the
  counter-metric, and it is as load-bearing as the target.
Lens assumptions: Assumptions, marked as such (unreviewed)
  Scan for claims about users, volumes or the market that are stated as fact. Mark
  each one inline as `[ASSUMPTION: …]` so a later reader can tell what was known
  from what was guessed — the two look identical in prose six months on.
----- BEGIN prd/prd rev e3116b43b6f5 -----
## Document Purpose

<!-- two or three sentences - what this document decides, for whom, and the verdict in one breath -->

## Vision

<!-- the problem and why now - every factual claim cited, every guess marked as one -->

## Target Users

<!-- who this serves, in their words - the jobs they are hiring it for, who it deliberately does not serve, and the journeys they actually narrated -->

## Glossary

<!-- the ubiquitous language - term, one line each; a synonym introduced downstream is a defect -->

## Features

<!-- what the product does and how you would know it works - group by capability under sub-headings, never by how it is built; link requirement ids (req- slugs) inline, coverage is a graph check -->

## Non-Goals

<!-- what this deliberately does not do, each with the reason - the "let me also add this nearby thing" firewall -->

## Deferred

<!-- not in this version, and what would bring it back - distinct from a permanent non-goal -->

## Success Metrics

<!-- how we know it worked - a baseline, a target, a date and the source someone queries; and what must NOT worsen while they move -->

## Roadmap

<!-- what lands in what order, and what each order buys - omit it where a separate plan owns sequencing -->

## Alternatives & Recommendation

<!-- what else was on the table and what each would cost, then the verdict and what would disconfirm it -->

## Open Questions

<!-- known unknowns, each with who would answer it and what it blocks -->

## Assumptions Index

<!-- every assumption this document proceeds on, and the consequence if it is wrong -->

## Grounding Evidence

<!-- the audit appendix - what was read to write this, and when -->
----- END prd/prd rev e3116b43b6f5 -----

== requirement/req-invoice-totals  path knowledge/requirements/req-invoice-totals.md  rev 15dfd18b9df0
Lens single: One statement (unreviewed)
  Does the body state exactly one thing? An "and", a bulleted list of behaviours, or
  two sentences each with their own verb mean this is two requirements. Split it —
  two records each with their own `realized_in` are worth more than one that cannot
  be satisfied or violated as a unit.
Lens testable: Observable from outside (unreviewed)
  Could someone who did not write this tell whether the system satisfies it, without
  reading the implementation? If the only way to check is to open the code, this is
  describing a design, not stating a requirement.
Lens measurable: A number and its measurement (unreviewed)
  Look for the value, the unit, where it is measured, over what window, and what
  enforces it. Flag "gracefully", "robust", "scalable", "efficient", "fast",
  "user-friendly" — an adjective is not a bound. "p99 under 200 ms at the API edge,
  measured over a rolling hour, alerted on in the latency dashboard" is.
Lens realized: Where it is satisfied (unreviewed)
  `realized_in` is the only edge tying a rule to the code that satisfies it, and a
  requirement without one is unfinished by design. If no component satisfies it yet,
  that is the honest answer and the gap is the point — but check that it is not
  simply unlinked. If a use case depends on this rule, name it in `use_cases`: a
  rule no flow needs is either global or unowned, and the graph should say which.
----- BEGIN requirement/req-invoice-totals rev 15dfd18b9df0 -----
<!-- one statement, in the present tense, of what must hold. EARS reads well here: "When <trigger>, the system shall <response>." Say what is measured and what enforces it — a `non-functional` requirement without a number is a wish. Link `realized_in` to the component that satisfies it. -->
----- END requirement/req-invoice-totals rev 15dfd18b9df0 -----

== requirement/req-order-cap  path knowledge/requirements/req-order-cap.md  rev 17d298123554
Lens single: One statement (unreviewed)
  Does the body state exactly one thing? An "and", a bulleted list of behaviours, or
  two sentences each with their own verb mean this is two requirements. Split it —
  two records each with their own `realized_in` are worth more than one that cannot
  be satisfied or violated as a unit.
Lens testable: Observable from outside (unreviewed)
  Could someone who did not write this tell whether the system satisfies it, without
  reading the implementation? If the only way to check is to open the code, this is
  describing a design, not stating a requirement.
Lens violation: What violating it looks like (unreviewed)
  A constraint is a rule the system must never break, so the useful question is what
  breaking it would look like from outside. If that cannot be described, this is
  probably a preference — and a preference belongs in an `adr`, with its reasoning.
Lens realized: Where it is satisfied (unreviewed)
  `realized_in` is the only edge tying a rule to the code that satisfies it, and a
  requirement without one is unfinished by design. If no component satisfies it yet,
  that is the honest answer and the gap is the point — but check that it is not
  simply unlinked. If a use case depends on this rule, name it in `use_cases`: a
  rule no flow needs is either global or unowned, and the graph should say which.
